{"id": "sllama0188", "pool": "sllama", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.69, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1955, "eot_read_ms": 29, "reference_answer": "Brah", "query": "Who is the supreme god in Hinduism?", "transcript": "Who is the supreme god in Hinduism?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Hinduism doesn't have one single \"supreme god\" that everyone agrees on. Instead, the ultimate reality is often called Brahman—a kind of infinite, formless divine essence. Different traditions within Hinduism focus on different deities as supreme, like Vishnu in Vaishnavism, Shiva in Shaivism, or Devi/Shakti in Shaktism. So it's more about various paths to the same ultimate truth rather than one specific godhead.", "expert_answer": "Hinduism does not have a single universally agreed “supreme god” across all traditions.\n\n- In many Hindu philosophies, the supreme reality is **Brahman** — the ultimate, formless divine reality.\n- In devotional traditions, the supreme deity may be identified as:\n  - **Vishnu** in Vaishnavism\n  - **Shiva** in Shaivism\n  - **Devi/Shakti** in Shaktism\n\nSo the best general answer is: **Brahman is the supreme ultimate reality, while specific traditions worship Vishnu, Shiva, or Devi as supreme.**", "expert_latency_s": 4.52, "stall_ms": 33, "relay_ms": 2935}
